---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { APP, PUBLISHER } from '../lib/site';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — HelloNotes"
  description="HelloNotes collects nothing: no account, no analytics, no tracking, no servers. Your notes are plain files on your own device."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  intro="Last updated: 18 October 2026"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1120px] px-6 py-12">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        {APP.name} is a <strong>local-first</strong> application published by{' '}
        {PUBLISHER.legalName} Your notes are stored as plain files on your own device, in a folder
        you select.{' '}
        <strong>
          We do not collect, transmit, sell, or have access to your notes or any personal data.
        </strong>
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <strong>In short:</strong> no account, no analytics, no tracking, no servers of ours.
          Your notes never leave your device unless you choose to send them somewhere yourself — to a
          service you sync with, or to Apple’s Private Cloud Compute if you choose Cloud (Apple) as
          the AI model. Otherwise what the AI features send anywhere is a web search, and the pages
          it then reads, when a question needs them. Both are described below.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. {APP.name} has no analytics, no telemetry, no advertising identifiers, and no user
        accounts. We do not operate any server that receives your data. Because we hold no data
        about you, there is nothing for us to disclose, share, or delete on request.
      </p>
      <p>
        A few of the app’s settings — appearance, the view you last used, the names of your
        daily-note, template and attachment folders — are kept in step between your devices
        through your own iCloud account, as many apps’ settings are. They contain none of your
        notes, and we cannot see them.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you write to us — a support request, a report of something the AI wrote, or any other
        email — we receive what you send, from your own mail app, and use it only to answer you.
        {APP.name} composes those messages for you to read and send; it sends nothing itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Your notes</h2>
      <p>
        Your notes are ordinary Markdown files in a folder on your device that you choose. They
        remain under your control and can be read or edited by any other application. {APP.name}{' '}
        reads and writes only the folders you explicitly open, using the OS's security-scoped file
        access.
      </p>

      <h2>Intelligence</h2>
      <p>
        The optional AI features — Summarise, Suggest Tags and Links, Rewrite, suggestions as you
        type, New Note from a Prompt and the Assistant — use Apple’s Foundation Models framework.
        By default they run on your device: on <strong>Local (Apple)</strong>, the Apple
        Intelligence model, or on an open model you choose to download and run on your device with
        MLX. Your note content is then processed locally and is <strong>not</strong> sent to us or
        to any AI service. {APP.name} has no third-party AI services and stores no AI credentials;
        API keys stored by earlier versions are deleted from your device.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the App Store version, Settings ▸ AI also offers <strong>Cloud (Apple)</strong>, Apple’s
        server model, which runs on Apple’s Private Cloud Compute. It is used only if you choose it
        — as the default model, or in the Assistant for one conversation. Then what a feature works
        on — your question, and the notes or passages it reads to answer it — is sent to Apple’s
        Private Cloud Compute to be processed. Apple does not store it, and it is not accessible to
        Apple; it never reaches us. Settings says so under the model whenever it is chosen, and so
        does the Assistant, in a conversation on it, and its answers and suggestions say which model
        made them.
        When Apple’s daily allowance for it is used up, the work is done on your device by
        Local (Apple) instead, until the allowance resets.
      </p>
      <p>
        Downloading an MLX model contacts Hugging Face to fetch the model’s files; none of your
        notes are sent. The Assistant can search the web — on its own, when a question needs it —
        and so can Research in New Note from a Prompt. The search terms the model writes go to
        DuckDuckGo, and they can include words from your question or from notes it has read; a page
        it reads is fetched from that page’s website.
      </p>

      <h2>Cloud storage and version control</h2>
      <p>
        If you keep a collection in iCloud Drive, Dropbox, Box, OneDrive or Google Drive, or connect
        one of those accounts directly, your files are handled by that service under its own privacy
        policy — the same as any other folder on your device. Similarly, if you use the built-in Git
        features with your own remote repository, your notes are sent only to the destination{' '}
        <em>you</em> configure. {APP.name} is not that destination and does not receive a copy.
      </p>

      <h2>Children’s privacy</h2>
      <p>{APP.name} does not collect data from anyone, including children, and is rated 4+.</p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the updated version will be posted on this page with a revised date.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.email}`}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>,
        or see the <a href={href('/support')}>support page</a>.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
